<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · War and march · 1825 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden (The Good Comrade)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="de">Der gute Kamerad</bdi> / <bdi lang="de-Latn">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German song from 1809 and 1825 in which a soldier mourns a friend lost in battle, played today at military funerals.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ludwig Uhland</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Friedrich Silcher, after the tune of a Swiss folk song</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German soldiers; later armies and veterans in many countries</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1809; music 1825</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Ludwig Uhland (1809); music by Friedrich Silcher (1825))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Der gute Kamerad&quot; (&quot;The Good Comrade&quot;), better known by its opening line as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ich-hatt-einen-kameraden">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden</a>&quot; (&quot;I had a comrade&quot;), is a German soldiers' lament. The German romantic poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-uhland">Ludwig Uhland</a> wrote the words in 1809; their immediate inspiration was the sending of troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> against the Tyrolean Rebellion. In 1825 the composer of Lieder Friedrich Silcher set them to music, on the tune of a Swiss folk song, in honour of those who had fallen in the more recent Wars of Liberation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s French Imperial Army.</p><p>The words describe the loss of a friend in combat without any political or national cause attached, and twice move from the past tense into the present, as a memory returns. Because of this the song was never tied to one country or one ideology. Through the 19th, 20th and 21st centuries it was sung across the political spectrum, right and left, and it was translated for armed forces in other languages, among them French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Spanish and Japanese. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-foreign-legion">French Foreign Legion</a> adopted it as early as the 19th century, and the Chilean Armed Forces and the National Army of Colombia also use it. In 1880 the scholar Heymann Steinthal described a variant he had heard a housemaid sing, which he thought improved on Uhland's text by evoking the military idea of a fated bullet.</p><p>German emigrants took it with them. Around St. Cloud, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a>, settled in the 1850s by Catholic immigrants invited by the missionary Fr. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> Pierz, the local historian Fr. Colman J. Barry recorded that German-American veterans of the Union Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> would stand and sing it, with tears, in honour of fallen friends at parish feast-day picnics and old-country festivals. Ernst Busch used its tune for his Spanish Civil War song on the death of Hans Beimler, and in 1966 the playwright Carl Zuckmayer took one of its lines as the title of his autobiography, published in English as A Part of Myself.</p><p>The song still has a ceremonial place in the German and Austrian armed forces and is part of every military funeral, a tradition dating from at least around 1871. It is played at the funerals of Austrian firefighters, of volunteer firefighters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> and at remembrance ceremonies of the Schützenbund there, at the funerals of members of student fraternities (Studentenverbindungen), and sometimes at civilian funerals, mostly of people who had served. On 22 May 2009 a Flemish band played it on Highland bagpipes and drums at a joint Belgian, British and German memorial at the German war cemetery of Langemark in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, where more than 44,000 German and two British soldiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> lie. It is played on the trumpet at the wreath-laying at the Neue Wache in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on Volkstrauertag, and every 20 July at the Memorial to the German Resistance in the Bendlerblock, in honour of the officers who plotted to kill Hitler, whom the Bundeswehr has honoured since its founding in the 1950s.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A soldier remembers a friend lost in combat, with the grief and the guilt of the one who survived. The mood is quiet and mournful rather than warlike, and the shifts into the present tense make the loss feel as if it were happening again.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I had a comrade,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Einen bessern findst du nicht.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A better one you will not find.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Trommel schlug zum Streite,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The drum beat for battle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er ging an meiner Seite</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He walked at my side</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">In gleichem Schritt und Tritt.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the same step and stride.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Eine Kugel kam geflogen:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A bullet came flying:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gilt's mir oder gilt es dir?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is it meant for me, or is it meant for you?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ihn hat es weggerissen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It has torn him away,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er liegt zu meinen Füßen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He lies at my feet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Als wär's ein Stück von mir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As if it were a piece of me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Will mir die Hand noch reichen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He still wants to reach me his hand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Derweil ich eben lad.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>While I am just loading.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Kann dir die Hand nicht geben,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I cannot give you my hand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Bleib du im ew'gen Leben</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Stay you, in eternal life,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mein guter Kamerad!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My good comrade!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ludwig Uhland (1809); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ich_hatt'_einen_Kameraden" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (1)</summary><ul><li>line 3.1: the subject 'he' is left out in the German ('Will mir die Hand noch reichen').</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hans Beimler (Ernst Busch)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, a Spanish Civil War song on the death of Hans Beimler</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Autobiography of Carl Zuckmayer (English title A Part of Myself)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a line of the song taken as the title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Translations for the French, Dutch, Spanish and Japanese armed forces</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Swiss folk song</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its tune was the basis of Silcher's melody</span></li></ul></section>
